::SPEECH_U02_Pretest_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_PT_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which speech focus is clearest?
{
~A. Presentations are things.#This is too vague to guide a speech.
=B. Organized preparation helps speakers deliver clearer presentations.#Correct. This is specific and can guide an informative speech.
~C. School has many topics.#This does not give a clear speaking focus.
~D. I might talk about something.#This is unclear and unfocused.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Pretest_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_PT_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which set best explains an informative point?
{
~A. Topic only.#A topic alone does not explain information.
~B. A joke and a conclusion.#This does not develop an informative point.
~C. Random facts only.#Random facts do not create clear explanation.
=D. Point, support, and reasoning.#Correct. This gives the idea, evidence or detail, and explanation.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Pretest_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_PT_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: Note cards help speakers remember the order of ideas. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
=A. Note cards can help speakers keep ideas in order.#Correct. This restates the source accurately.
~B. Speakers should avoid notes.#This contradicts the source.
~C. Note cards make ideas unnecessary.#This changes the meaning.
~D. Order does not matter.#This contradicts the source.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Pretest_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_PT_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which speech product is most complete?
{
~A. A title only.#A title alone is incomplete.
~B. A copied paragraph only.#This is not a speech-ready plan.
=C. Focus, main points, support, transitions, and conclusion.#Correct. These are key parts of a quality speech product.
~D. A random list of words.#Random words do not show format.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Pretest_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_PT_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.01
Question:
Which structure is best for explaining steps?
{
=A. Chronological order#Correct. Steps are clearest in time order.
~B. Random order#Random order makes steps hard to follow.
~C. Hidden structure#Hidden structure does not guide the audience.
~D. Unrelated details#Unrelated details do not explain steps.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Pretest_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_PT_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.04
Question:
Which sentence follows standard English?
{
~A. my outline show three planning points.#This has capitalization and agreement errors.
~B. my outline shows the speech points.#This needs capitalization before it follows standard English.
~C. My outline show three points.#This has a subject-verb agreement error.
=D. My outline shows three points.#Correct. This sentence follows standard English conventions.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Pretest_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_PT_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which phrase uses precise academic speech vocabulary?
{
~A. talking stuff#This is vague and informal.
=B. coherent focus#Correct. This is precise vocabulary for speech planning.
~C. things to say#This is too general because it does not use precise speech-planning vocabulary.
~D. whatever words#This is informal and unclear.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Pretest_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_PT_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which transition best connects organization to support?
{
~A. Next thing.#This is vague because it does not explain how one organized idea leads to the support that follows.
~B. I am done now.#This does not connect ideas.
=C. After organizing the points, the speaker should add support that explains each idea.#Correct. This transition shows the relationship between ideas.
~D. Anyway, support.#This is too casual and unclear.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Pretest_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_PT_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which plan has coherent focus?
{
~A. Focus: preparation; points: lunch, weather, sports.#These points do not support the focus.
=B. Focus: preparation; points: outline, support, rehearsal.#Correct. All points support preparation.
~C. Focus: speeches; points: cafeteria, shoes, music.#These points are unrelated.
~D. Focus: notes; points: weekends, games, food.#These points do not support the focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_Pretest_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_PT_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which statement best describes Unit 2 readiness?
{
=A. A strong informative speech plan has focus, organization, support, transitions, and quality format.#Correct. This combines the major Unit 2 skills.
~B. A speech plan only needs a topic.#A topic alone is incomplete.
~C. Paraphrase means changing the source meaning.#Paraphrase must keep the meaning accurate.
~D. Support should be unrelated to the point.#Support must connect to the point.
}